            "figures": [
              {
                "value": "40 percent",
                "label": "Disability Eligibility Threshold",
                "source": "Two changes matter. On 15 October 2024 the Supreme Court held in Omkar Ramchandra Gond v Union of India that a disability of 40 percent or more does not by itself bar MBBS, and that a board must record whether the disability would actually prevent the course. On 27 July 2026 the NMC issued guidelines that judge functional ability, with an Eligibility Certificate that can say Eligible, Eligible with Reasonable Accommodation, or Ineligible with reasons.",
                "text": "Two changes matter. On 15 October 2024 the Supreme Court held in Omkar Ramchandra Gond v Union of India that a disability of 40 percent or more does not by itself bar MBBS, and that a board must record whether the disability would actually prevent the course. On 27 July 2026 the NMC issued guidelines that judge functional ability, with an Eligibility Certificate that can say Eligible, Eligible with Reasonable Accommodation, or Ineligible with reasons."
              }
            ],
            "sourceSectionId": "routes",
            "sourceNeedIndex": 5
          },
          {
            "needId": "proof:10",
            "type": "proof",
            "heading": "College Money and the 10 Percent Rule",
            "kind": "figures",
            "figures": [
              {
                "value": "10 percent",
                "label": "Education Budget Share",
                "source": "Our strongest suggestion: spend about 10 percent of the family's whole education budget on college fees.",
                "text": "Our strongest suggestion: spend about 10 percent of the family's whole education budget on college fees."
              },
              {
                "value": "10 lakh",
                "label": "Education Budget Amount",
                "source": "On a 10 lakh rupee budget that is about 1 lakh rupees for the degree, and the other 9 lakh rupees must cover living costs, devices, internet, short courses, projects and future learning. Above about 20 percent, ask for hard evidence that the college gives something you cannot get otherwise, such as a licence your route legally needs. Borrow for a degree only when a legally required credential leaves no other way.",
                "text": "On a 10 lakh rupee budget that is about 1 lakh rupees for the degree, and the other 9 lakh rupees must cover living costs, devices, internet, short courses, projects and future learning. Above about 20 percent, ask for hard evidence that the college gives something you cannot get otherwise, such as a licence your route legally needs. Borrow for a degree only when a legally required credential leaves no other way."
